Many thanks to James Holtman, Patrick 
Burns, and David Quinn. Here are the suggestions:

1) Can you explain a little better what your problem is.  Your 
comparisons, and the conditions, will always cause the "stop" message.  
What are the conditions that you want to have evaluated?  If there are 
equalities in the data, then you would have to include this as part of you 
test.

By the way, you are using "&" instead of the logical 
"&&".  Might this be causing your problem?  "&" works with 
vectors. -James Holtman-

Details were sent as requested. James replied 
with this

2) I would do something like this:
if (A  && 
b==c && c==d && d==e){
      
Selection <- sample(1:4,1)
      ... based on the 
value returned (1 -> 4), merge the appropriate data
}

The other IF 
statements should then be
if (A && b>=c && c>=d 
&& d>=e) ...... 
-James Holtman-

3) From your message it is 
unclear to me what you would like to happen given the state that you are 
experiencing. But it sounds like you have a very complex logical structure -- 
one trick is to use "switch" with a condition that you have computed. The fjj4 
function on page 208 of S Poetry is an example. By the way, in "if" statements 
you should use "&&" rather than "&". Good luck, Patrick 
Burns

4) In case someone hasn't already answered ... looks like the 
"correct" reaction to your data is to stop since none of the "if" conditions is 
true.  You may want ">=" rather than ">" inequalities to get your 
desired behavior. -- David

 

Best wishes for a happy New Year full of joy, peace and hope to you 
all.

 

Ray. 

