From parkhurs at indiana.edu  Tue Jan  7 18:03:04 2003
From: parkhurs at indiana.edu (David Parkhurst)
Date: Tue, 07 Jan 2003 13:03:04 -0500
Subject: Replies to: Correlated explanatory variables in tree regression
Message-ID: <000101c2b677$3604e890$a2414842@spea.indiana.edu>

I asked: "I think I recently read somewhere that, in contrast to multiple 
regression,
tree regression is not negatively affected by correlated explanatory variables. 
 (In
fact, what I think I read is that some such correlation is actually desirable.) 
Can
anyone tell me where I might have read about these questions?  (I've searched 
all the
sources I can recall where I might have done so.)  Other comments would be 
welcome
too."

Here are the replies I received.  Thanks to all. (I have more reading to do<g>.)

>From Bert Gunter: Don't know where you read it and don't know whether you 
should call
it desirable or not. What I can say is the many correlated variables will lead 
to
"quite" different tree topologies when the responses are only "a little" 
perturbed,
just as in multiple regression (where the regression coefficients will change a 
lot).
While predictions will NOT be much affected (if you don't extrapolate too much),
interpretation clearly will be, which is one of the features of tree-based 
regression
much beloved by its devotees. To be clear: interpretation of the tree topologies
(e.g., variable importance) is highly problematic with correlated variables. 
And I'm
being generous here.

You might have seen something by Leo Breiman claiming that "the more (possibly
correlated) variables, the better" for his "Random Forests" classification 
procedures.
This is an "ensemble method" based on trees, but, like all ensemble methods, 
does not
produce nicely interpretable results (the aim and claim is better predictions).
Recently, my colleagues Andy Liaw and Matt Wiener have implemented this in 
S-Plus and
R via the randomForest() function. They have written a nice overview of this in 
the
most recent (December 2002) R-News, available online from CRAN.

>From Bob Pruzek:  As someone who has used trees extensively in recent years, I 
can
report that trees work fine in most situations where there are 
interdependencies among
explanatory variables. And most of what I've read about trees supports this 
view; but
there can be complications, depending especially on the particular tree 
algorithims
used; One starting point is: http://www.statsoft.com/textbook/stclatre.html   
and for
more references see: C:\cee.sevrl.psa.stuff\psa.related\BIBLIOGRAPHIC
CITATIONs.classif.regr.trees.htm

IMHO there is no particular reason why trees should work better when 
interdependencies
increase among explanatory variables, but in practical situations one does not 
usually
control such matters, except perhaps through sampling, so the important point 
seems to
me that trees can work effectively whether or not explanatory variables are
interdependent.  (Note that the term correlation, were it to be interpreted as 
linear
correlation, is much less apt than the term interdependent.)

>From Tony Plate:  Whether or not correlation among explanatory variables is 
harmful
depends  largely on whether you are trying to do prediction or inference, and 
not so
much on the type of model

Here's my limited understanding: If you are using a tree model for prediction, 
then
correlation can help in  the presence of missing values, because if there are 
many
correlated  values, the model-building program is likely able to find good 
surrogate
variables for splits (this only applies if your tree-building program looks  for
surrogate variables, e.g., CART).

If you are using a linear regression model for prediction, then as long as  the 
values
of the explanatory variables that you want to predict for is  drawn from the 
same
joint distribution that you fitted the model on,  correlations among explanatory
variables don't harm the predictions.

If you are using *any* kind of model for inference (i.e., trying to  estimate 
the
strength and/or nature of the relationship between individual  explanatory 
variables
and the dependent variable) then correlations among  explanatory variables will 
in
general reduce the level of confidence you  can have in the inferences.

