From Neal.Alexander at lshtm.ac.uk  Wed Jan  8 18:00:28 2003
From: Neal.Alexander at lshtm.ac.uk (Neal Alexander)
Date: Wed, 08 Jan 2003 18:00:28 +0000
Subject: summary of responses Re: mean of Gompertz
Message-ID: <se1c6745.080@s-webmail.lshtm.ac.uk>

Thanks to all those who replied.  To summarize:

   Several people pointed out that the incomplete gamma function is related to 
the CDF of the gamma distribution.  Specifically, I would multiply the CDF from 
pgamma(shape, ...) by gamma(shape), cancelling the gamma(shape) in the 
denominator of the density.  Unfortunately, comparing the gamma density with 
the incomplete gamma function 
(http://mathworld.wolfram.com/IncompleteGammaFunction.html), it seems I need 
the shape parameter in the former, ie  the 1st param in the latter, to be zero, 
which makes it blow up.  

   In the meantime I had investigated using integrate() on the exponential 
integral directly, and on the incomplete gamma function.  The latter seemed to 
be more robust.  Both blew up (components diverging in different directions) as 
the Gompertz tends to the exponential (ln(c) tends to zero), but this can be 
crudely trapped by setting the mean to 1/B below a certain value of ln(c).  
Finally, I had forgotten that we can, very nicely, set the upper integration 
limit to Inf, so that aspect was not a problem.  

   However, Ravi Varadhan kindly sent me a DLL for the exponential integal, 
which was developed for R but which works on my S+ 6.1/Win 2000 setup.

Neal

Neal Alexander, PhD, Senior Lecturer in Medical Statistics & Epidemiology
   Infectious Disease Epidemiology Unit, Dept of Infectious & Tropical Diseases
   London School of Hygiene&Tropical Medicine,Keppel St,London WC1E 7HT,UK
   neal.alexander at lshtm.ac.uk; tel (+44) 20 7927 2826; fax 20 7636 8739

>>> "Neal Alexander" <Neal.Alexander at lshtm.ac.uk> 01/07/03 03:27pm >>>

Dear All,

I want to calculate the mean of a Gompertz distribution, ie one with hazard 
function h(x) = B*(c^x) = B*exp(x*lnc).  I found this surprisingly difficult to 
track down, but in an actuarial paper 
(http://www.soa.org/library/arch/1990-99/ARCH98V27.pdf) I found that it is:

   exp(B/lnc)*(-1/lnc)*Ei(-B/lnc)

where Ei is the exponential integral: Ei(-a) = -a*integral_1^Inf {exp(-ax) * 
lnx dx}.  I found (http://mathworld.wolfram.com/ExponentialIntegral.html) that 
this integral can easily be obtained from the _incomplete_ gamma function, but 
not from the normal gamma function provided in S+.  

I can estimate this integral using integrate() but, as well as being 
CPU-intensive, it would need some care in choosing the upper integration limit.

Does anyone have a neater solution?

Thanks,

Neal

