5. Response
In general, this is impossible because there are too many possible
combinations (combinatorial explosion) for more than relatively few
predictors.
For small numbers of predictors, the s function leaps() will do this --
but
you will find that there are many nearly equivalent models. This is the
nature of modeling, and thee is no good empirical method to choose among
them, althouh you can get hints through a variety of techniques.
I believe leaps() provides the Cp statistic. The Mallows Cp plot (which
might be easily generatable in S-Plus -- check Help) is one classic way of
choosing among leaps() output.

This is a huge area in the statistical literature -- try checking with your
local statistician.

-- Bert
......................
6. Response
Thanks you Frank Harrel,
> I know your book on modelling strategies, which generally
warns of any stepwise procedure, just like Quinn and Keogh
(Quinn, G.P. and M.J. Keough.  2002.  Experimental design and
data analysis for biologists.) do.
> So, you would refer to a technique that is trying all
possible combinations still a stepwise procedure?  Would you
be able to explain a little further?

Yes, it's very much a type of stepwise procedure, and one
that can have more problems than ordinary stepwise.  It has
huge multiple comparison problems.  See

@ARTICLE{roe91pre,
  author = {Roecker, Ellen B.},
  year = 1991,
  title = {Prediction error and its estimation for
subset-selected models},
  journal = {Technometrics},
  volume = 33,
  pages = {459-468}
}
@ARTICLE{man70why,
  author = {Mantel, Nathan},
  year = 1970,
  title = {Why stepdown procedures in variable selection},
  journal = {Technometrics},
  volume = 12,
  pages = {621-625},
  annote = {variable selection; collinearity}

> Which main strategy (besides specific hypothesis
formulation) would you recommend?

My book goes into detail about that.  Main message is to do
one of the following:

1. postulate a model, fit it, and go home
2. postulate a model, use penalized MLE to get more stable
estimates, and quit
3. use a data reduction technique to group related variables,
and fit cluster scores in the regression.  The data reduction
technique may not use the response variable.

Frank
