Hi Brian,

Thanks for your reply and insights.  I'll address your last, and best, 
question first: why I want to do this.  There seemed to be a fair bit of 
interest in this expressed by others, so I beg leave of the list to wander 
off-topic.

I'm interested in ensemble weather forecasting problems.  You may be 
familiar with the topic. Briefly, numerical models of the atmosphere are 
known to display sensitive dependence to initial conditions.  Because the 
initial conditions provided these models contain errors, the resulting 
forecasts contain errors.  However, we have some knowledge about the errors 
contained in these initial conditions, and we sometimes have knowledge 
about how the models respond to errors on certain scales and originating in 
certain locations.  Hence, to estimate some measure of the predictability 
of weather on any given day, an ensemble of numerical models, each started 
with a different set of initial conditions, is run.  Ideally, statistics 
about the different solutions will yield insights into how probable certain 
weather scenarios are. Until recently, ensemble forecasting has been used 
primarily for long-range (7-10 day), hemispheric-scale forecasts, and 
primarily in the UK and Europe.

I'm particularly interested in extracting probabilities of convective 
storms from an ensemble of numerical models that represent a very fine 
scale over a region the size of the US East of the Rocky Mountains, and 
that are run out only 24-48 h. One way to begin to examine such 
probabilities is through KDEs. I say this because the errors displayed by 
such ensembles are clearly non-Gaussian, and a KDE seems, at first blush, a 
straightforward approach to an initial investigation.

As to sample size, we can generate fairly large samples, 100 to 1000 points 
per day, depending on how widespread thunderstorms are. But after 
re-reading Silverman's book, we're unlikely to have enough data for more 
than 3 or 4 dimensions.  Silverman ("Density Estimation for Statistics and 
Data Analysis," Silverman, B.W., 1986) contains a numerical exercise where 
the needed sample size to accurately estimate a multidimensional unit 
normal density at a single point (0) increases to 768 for 5 dimensions 
(about 10^6 for 10 dimensions).  This is not as innocuous as it sounds, 
because he warns that this number is likely to be much higher if accurate 
estimates in the tails are needed, and it will certainly get large for 
non-normal distributions.

Hence, my query.

I'm happy to discuss this further off-list with anyone who is interested.

Thanks much for you insights,

Kim Elmore

