Message-ID: <Pine.LNX.4.44.0301170816380.2578-100000@gannet.stats>

Both approaches are extensible, but work much less well in kD unless their
models are reasonable approximations to the underlying density. And to
know the latter, you need a non-parametric estimate to compare with,
Without some idea of the sort of density expected, *I* would not be 
prepared to offer advice on suitable methods.

10,000 observations for a 1D log-normal is data-rich:  10,000 observations
for a multimodal density in 3D is a completely different matter. You can't
even begin to count normals, as in kD they have lots of parameters each,
and you have to be able to fit the mixture of normals somehow.  By MLE, it
is a high-dimensional optimization problem with many local maxima, hard
enough to do for a simple 1D problem, and where do the starting values for
the optimization come from?

Challenge to `super.gamer': show us S code that can reliably fit mixtures
of normals to 2D data, including choosing the number of normal components,
to, e.g. the 2D examples kde2d is used for in MASS and in my PRNN book
(where the interest is in the tails for use in discrimination).  
Preferably in less than 5mins computation.

The `curse of dimensionality' goes back at least to Bellman.

