Why use S? There are already software listed in Geoffrey McLachlan's Finite 
Mixture model book (Published Oct 2000) that can already do the job. Mr. 
Ripley, why don't you download it and translate it into S and see if how much 
slower/faster it will run. And perhaps you can include it in the next yearly 
edition of your book. BTW, MLE is not used in Finite Mixture Model. One needs 
EM or MCMC for inference. They is no software written in SPLUS, perhaps because 
SPLUS is slow and has problems with large datasets? Challenge to Mr. Ripley : 
try running a computationally intensive MCMC routine on a massive dataset with 
SPLUS and see if it will crash. (Believe or Not?).

Nonparametric methods requires a huge amount of data. Once should avoid such 
method when a semiparametric alternative is available, as it requires 
substantially less data. Finite Mixture Models are flexible enough for most 
situation. I have not seen a density estimation problem where Finite Mixture 
Models cannot handle. Silverman's book is a bit old (published in the 80's, I 
think). The popularity of Finite Mixture Models has grown since the advent of 
EM. They has been alot of published work in the this subject in recent years. 

-richard

> Both approaches are extensible, but work much less well in kD unless their
