Dear 
all,

 

I am doing a 
regression with lots of numerical variables on S+ 6.1.

Now I want to 
include the product of two variables a and b in my 
regression:

 

lm(y~c+a*b,data="">

 

and I find that 
the result is very different from what I get if I define D$ab<-D$a*D$b and 
try

 

lm(y~c+ab,data="">

 

So my questions: 
what does * do and, more importantly, how can I include the product of two 
variables without first defining them in my dataframe.

 

Thanks for any 
help,

 

Harald

From Hoogstrate at POINTLOGIC.NL  Mon Jan 20 15:05:49 2003
From: Hoogstrate at POINTLOGIC.NL (Harald Hoogstrate)
Date: Mon, 20 Jan 2003 16:05:49 +0100
Subject: Re: product of numeric variables in lm
In-Reply-To: <55670B277149944B9A64D5F5CF25C90604FFDD@ntserver.pointlogic.nl>
Message-ID: <55670B277149944B9A64D5F5CF25C9061E98BF@ntserver.pointlogic.nl>

OK, 
three answers within 5 minutes, must be a speedrecord.

 

This one from Beth Atkinson best explains 
the S+ behaviour:

 

"a*b is a + b + a:b

The definition you used of 
D$a*D$b only includes the interaction term and not the main effects. 

If you only want 
the interaction, use 
lm(y~c+a:b,data="" 
class=987350115-20012003>"

 

Thanks 
all,

Harald

 

 

 

 

