Hi, Suna, 

I don't know exactly how all this works, I've just learned that it's good to
tell the domain host so they can take action.

The spammer seems to have found the S-news e-mail address so it came to us
via that route.  Sometimes spammers use professional directories (like
American Statistical Association for example) to get a list of addresses to
spam; a listserve address is the equivalent of a mailing list - if the
listserve hides the subscriber list then the spammer might use the
"unsubscribe" trick to confirm the subscriber e-mails (those who try to
unsubscribe).

Yahoo offers this information about how spammers might get e-mails from
Yahoo groups: http://help.yahoo.com/help/us/groups/members/members-03.html 

The group the message came from may actually be a Yahoo group (and if so
Yahoo will shut it down) or the spammer may have hijacked another e-mail
address (in which case Yahoo will work with the domain that it really came
from to get them to shut it down).  The spammer will probably try again by
opening other fake groups or e-mail accounts, so it's a constant battle.
Best you can do is to report it to the domain if you can find the time, or
hope others will.

So, don't be embarrassed (although the content was awful and the spammer
very tricky with that "dream house" advertisement at the beginning) - most
people know spam when they see it (even when it's not porn) - and just fight
back.

Laurie

